In article <734635644snz@granta.demon.co.uk> malwen@granta.demon.co.uk writes:
>I can see the seasoned players are keen for new challenges -
>but please remember that some of us are very new and still have a lot
>to learn. 
>Until I began to read this group I was doing well if I got to 
>Dungeon level 7 as there are so many ways to die if one is inexperienced.
>Don't make it too hard for newcomers.

Well, that's just the problem.

The beginning part of the game (after about level 3 or 4) is
really, really tough.  Your character is still a wimp at that point,
and a chance encounter with a leucrotta, horde of orcs, or wand of
sleep spells inevitable death.  Probably 85% of my characters croak
in the early stages of the game.

Once your character has gotten past the mines, the castle, and the
quest, though, he is generally very well equipped, has luck maxed out,
has the worst monsters genocided, and has tons of hit points.  Nothing
can stand up to that.

The rest of the game, though, lasts a long time.  You have to wade
through at least 20 large levels of Hell.  It gets boring.  About the
only thing that can kill you is a drowning (by a Kraken, or such) or a
dumb move.  The interesting part comes at the end, with the elemental
and astral planes.

The game would be better if the levels of Hell were tougher but fewer.
Making liches un-genocidable would certainly do the trick.

